  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-39533",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [1,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-b=1\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{x-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(5,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =a\\sqrt{5-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =2a+2 \\\\ 2a & =2 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}+2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2=2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
